A||||15||ezsurf||Transmission won't go in gear
Z||000000||ezsurf||07-06-99||08:06 PM||||<br>After just completing an engine rebuild with a new clutch, new pressure plate, new throw-out bearing and resurfacing the flywheel, the car won't go in gear.  The clutch pedal engages smoothly, and you can see the plunger move, but the car won't go in gear with the engine running.  The car had a new clutch master cylinder installed just before I bought it.  Before the rebuild, the car shifted as smooth as glass. The car hasn't been driven in three months, and the clutch master cylinder was pressure bled at the garage where it was installed.  Where's a good place to start?
Z||000001||four50four||07-06-99||08:11 PM||||what do you mean it won't go in gear? will the shifter not move or does it die when you put it in gear with the clutch engaged?
Z||000002||ezsurf||07-06-99||09:18 PM||||When the engine is running, and you engage the clutch and try to put the transmission in gear, all you do is grind gears.  It appears that the clutch is not fully engaging.  I don't know if this hydraulic clutch has any adjustments.  If it doesn't, all I know to do is top off the fluid and bleed the system.
Z||000003||theogre||07-06-99||09:56 PM||||there is an old thread called clutch info uncovered. dig it out and read it carefully. We cover allot of ground there that no one want's to retype.<p>There are a souple other thread since that one that cover things about shifter linkages, and transmission internals.
Z||000004||theogre||07-06-99||09:59 PM||||being that is was recently appart...... if it wasn't installed right you could have input shaft binding in the pilot bearing. You could also have a bent clutch disk which could cause draging even if the rest of the system is perfect.<p>First bet..... they didn't bleed it as well as they thought. Fiero will not tolerate even the smallest air bubbles.
Z||000005||ezsurf||07-06-99||10:40 PM||||Thanks for the input.  I read the thread on chutch info a while back.  Because the car shifted so smoothly before I rebuilt the engine, and because it had a new clutch master cylinder, I was hoping I would avoid all the problems I read about this clutch. I guess it's my turn at bat. 
Z||000006||ChadMan||07-07-99||07:13 AM||||ezsurf, are you 100% certain your slave cylinder is good?  I had a similar problem to yours and had a buddy push the clutch pedal down while I watched the slave cylinder.  I saw the slave push the actuating rod out and assumed it was a mechanical problem.  I had the clutch and throwout bearing replaced, but the car wasn't much better.  I replaced the slave cylinder and the car has shifted like a new one ever since.  My new slave cylinder pushes the actuating rod out about 2" further than the old one did.  Hope this helps.
Z||000007||PaUL||07-07-99||07:55 PM||||Could it be that whoever did the clutch job put the clutch disc in the wrong way? One side of the disc is marked "flywheel side". If it goes in wrong, it wont release. 
Z||000008||Carrolles||07-08-99||11:12 AM||||It sounds like you had too much surface removed from the flywheel when it was resurfaced.  Fieros have very little room for this.  Ed Parks recommends not resurfacing for this reason unless you absolutely have to due to gouges etc. in the flywheel.
Z||000009||PaUL||07-08-99||02:06 PM||||Yes I agree, didnt see the part about resurfacing the flywheel earlier. I just found out how tight the space is between the center of clutch disc and flywheel, read my post called <b>My Clutch Nightmare</b>. The springs are probably pressed up against the flywheel. The only fix for this I can think of is to take the flywheel to a machine shop and put it on a lathe to grind down the center slightly.
Z||000010||ezsurf||07-08-99||02:52 PM||||Thanks for all the input.  I'll take a closer look at it this saturday.  There is one thing I need to know.  If I remember correcty, there is no adjustment on this clutch.  Is this correct?  If so, I will re-check the connections, pressure bleed the clutch, and replace the slave cylinder if it doesn't work.  The old clutch disk was pretty worn.  The flywheel didn't look like they machined off a whole lot of material.  The new clutch disk was about 3mm thicker than the old one.  I'm also wondering if it's possible to get air in the system by simply unbolting the slave cylinder.  It doesn't look like the pedal is bent, and it shifted great before the change.  So, I don't believe the pedal is part of the problem. 
Z||000011||Phil||07-09-99||05:28 AM||||You say that your problems started right after you had some work done. I would stop payment on my check until it's fixed properly. Also if you have an aluminium clutch pedal it IS bent but you can't tell by looking at it while it is in the car.
Z||000012||theogre||07-09-99||10:33 PM||||DO NOT EVER alter the center of a fly wheel! It's a sure way to have the whole damn thing shatter.
Z||000013||ezsurf||07-10-99||05:04 PM||||I believe I've found the problem.  The actuating pin from the slave cylinder was not seated in the actuating lever socket.  It's been pouring down rain all day today, so I won't know if it's fixed until I get a chance to drive it.  If this does not fix it then I'm going to have quite a headache.
Z||000014||mwbackus||07-11-99||01:13 PM||||Good, at least now you are on the right track.  That $15 release lever is the primary cause of the clutch problem you describe.  It is probably loose (or cracked) where it attaches to the clutch shaft.<p>Mike
Z||000015||ezsurf||07-12-99||07:35 PM||||It shifts good now!  The problem was the actuating pin, it was not seated in the lever socket.  The engine hits a good lick as well.  The Crane cam was a good choice.  Now as soon as I get this massive exhaust leak fixed, it won't sound like I'm running NASCAR.  But it sounds cool!
